@meta
id: 05-01
title: One word at a time
chapter: 05
type: lesson
skills: word-w
par: 26
minutes: 4
vscode: Ctrl-Right, or clicking where you want to be
open: release-notes.md

@brief
`l` moves one character. On a line of prose that is twelve presses to get
past a single word, and you have been holding a key down to do it.

Words are the unit you actually think in. Vim has a motion for them, and
it lands on the first character of the next word every time.

The release note is missing a qualifier. The tax total shown at checkout
is an estimate, not the final figure, and legal want that said. Make the
first line read:

    The checkout page now shows the estimated tax total before payment.

Leave the second line alone.

@keys
| `w` | forward to the start of the next word |
| `i` | insert to the left of the cursor |
| `Esc` | back to normal mode |
| `:wq` | write and quit |

@hints
The new word goes in front of `tax`, so get the cursor onto the `t` of
`tax` and insert there. Do not move a character at a time.
--
`w` jumps forward one word and lands on its first character. Press it
repeatedly and watch where the cursor stops.
--
Six `w` presses from the start of the line put you on `tax`. Then `i`,
type `estimated` and a space, `Esc`, `:wq`.

@solution
    wwwwww  i estimated <Esc>  :wq<Enter>

Notice exactly where `w` puts you: on the *first* character of the word,
which is where you want to be when something goes in front of it. That
pairing — `w` then `i` — inserts before a word and it is the most common
thing you will ever do with a word motion.

The jump ignores how long the words are. `checkout` and `the` cost one
press each. That is the whole point: you are moving by meaning, not by
distance, so the effort no longer scales with the size of the identifier
you are jumping over.

Your VS Code hand wants Ctrl-Right here. It does nearly the same thing,
but it needs a modifier, and it cannot be given a count. Both of those
matter within a chapter or two.

@start release-notes.md
The checkout page now shows the tax total before payment.
Nothing else on this screen changed.

@expected release-notes.md
The checkout page now shows the estimated tax total before payment.
Nothing else on this screen changed.
